== 18. Of the Mistress of the Family, or Goodwife of the House

The goodwife or mistress of the house, is a person which yieldeth help and assistance in government to the master of the family. For he is, as it were, the prince and chief ruler; she is the associate, not only in office and authority, but also in advise, and counsel unto him. 1. King. 17. 17. #emph[And after these things, the son of the woman, which was wife, or] #footnote[\[Bagnalath babbaijth.\] Domina domus.] #emph[mistress of the house, was sick.]

Her duty is two-fold.

First to govern the house, as much as concerneth her, in her place. 1. Tim. 5. 14. #emph[I will therefore that the younger women marry, and govern the house.] And that she doth three ways.

#set enum(numbering: "I.")

+ By exercising her self in some profitable employments, for the good of her charge, Prov. 31. 13. #emph[She seeketh wool, and flax, and laboureth cheerfully with her hands.]
+ By appointing her maids their work, and overseeing them therein. Vers. 15. #emph[She riseth whiles it is yet night, and giveth the portion to her household, and the ordinary to her maids.]
+ By ordering her children and servants in wisdom; partly by instruction, partly by admonition, when there is need. Vers. 26. #emph[She openeth her mouth with wisdom, and the law of grace is in her tongue.] vers. 28. #emph[Her children rise up, and call her blessed,] &c. Tit. 2. 5. #emph[That they be discreet, chaste, keeping at home,] &c. Act. 18. 26. #emph[Priscilla] in her house, with #emph[Aquila,] instructed #emph[Apollos,] in that wherein he was wanting, expounding unto him #emph[the way of God more perfectly. Gregory Nazianzen] in an Oration made for his father, saith, #emph[That his mother was not only an helper, but also a teacher or instructor, and guide unto his father in matters of religion & godliness.]

#emph[The second duty is, to give the portion of food unto her family, or cause it to be given in due season. Prov.] 31. 15. She riseth whiles it is yet night, and giveth the portion, or meat to her household. #emph[Thus did] Rebekah #emph[in her house. Gen.] 27. 9. Get thee now to the flock, & bring me thence two good kids of the goats, that I may make pleasant meat of them for thy father, such as he loveth.

#emph[Quest.] Whether may the goodwife, without the consent of the goodman, give and bestow the goods belonging to the family?

#emph[Ans.] I. There are some things which are proper to her self, and not part of her dowry which she brought unto her husband. And these she may claim as her own, because either they were reserved upon the match made between them, or else are peculiar unto her by their mutual consent, and of them she may lawfully give, without her husbands knowledge. II. The goods which are common to them both, are of another nature; and these she may not bestow, without his allowance, either express or at least general and implicit, specially when the giving of them doth or may grow in likelihood, to the hindrance, and prejudice of the state of the family.
